          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does she have to weigh herself every day?",
          "answer": "Daily weighing helps catch fluid buildup early. The reference says weight is a proxy for fluid, and it changes faster than symptoms do. A gain of a few pounds over two or three days, or a larger gain over a week, is usually fluid rather than body tissue. That is the standard reason to contact the care team before breathlessness or swelling appears (*Diet and fluid in heart failure: sodium, volume, and the daily weight*).\n\nThe reference also describes how to weigh consistently: use the same scale, at the same time of day (after waking and using the toilet, before eating), and write the number down so it can be compared from day to day (same source).\n\nThe reference also lists rapid weight gain over a few days as a reason for urgent assessment (*Heart failure*). The general reference notes that daily weights and recognizing early fluid retention are part of self-management (same source). It does not say what specific weight gain should trigger a call. Her care team can set that threshold for her.\n\nIf her weight rises quickly, or she has breathlessness at rest, chest pain, fainting, or confusion, get emergency help right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
